# Batch files check out with CRLF on every machine.
#
# Why, honestly: NOT because cmd.exe breaks without it. That was the reason I first
# wrote here, and I tested it rather than assert it -- an LF-only .bat containing a
# parenthesised multi-line block and `exit /b` ran identically to the CRLF one on
# Windows 11, same output, same exit code. Modern cmd.exe tolerates LF fine.
#
# The real reason is that without this the repo produces a DIFFERENT working tree
# depending on who clones it. There is no .gitattributes, core.autocrlf is true on
# the author's machine, so the blobs are stored LF and handed back as CRLF here --
# while a clone with autocrlf=false gets LF. That already showed up as drift: four
# of the five launchers sat on disk as CRLF and one as LF, for no reason anybody
# chose. eol=crlf makes the checkout deterministic and matches what a .bat is
# conventionally expected to be.
#
# So: defensive and tidying, not a fix for an observed break. If that distinction
# ever stops being recorded, someone will "fix" a bug that was never there.
#
# Nothing else is constrained on purpose. Forcing an eol on the .ps1 files would
# rewrite thousands of lines for no benefit, and PowerShell does not care.
*.bat text eol=crlf
